<p>Israel is actively considering a ceasefire with Lebanon after rare direct talks between Israeli and Lebanese representatives in Washington on April 15. Israeli officials said the security cabinet met to discuss the proposal. Several outlets said expectations were rising that a truce could come soon, though Israel’s cabinet ended one meeting without a final decision, a sign that diplomacy is moving forward but is not finished yet. President Trump said Israeli and Lebanese leaders were due to speak on Thursday, describing the contact as an effort to create “breathing room” after more than six weeks of war between Israel and Hezbollah. A possible Lebanon ceasefire matters beyond the immediate border fighting because several reports said ending that front had been a key obstacle in wider diplomacy tied to the US-Iran track, potentially opening the door to a broader easing of regional tensions.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/o6QNaDsV2uE6xmAvptmxzILfZp0Fk0xmW0eq9JMQhB5fnn_ZFJBeP3yA3nDBk30okEruxcH7wumvs_T-JUdYVYiY8V3y-4hSatXux37hjWWOlNpzmhv4Bs7vMkr-VtFj6Q9EdkKS2aJsgReyFfCeKyOKS_2VECpod4MhZ4-J16ISWErEATaI6CTc69m4GOCgi0-ND8Z8Q_SaapwusOs1O2tVuVsnWsqZBDLhk0ckWBdlBnf5yVK9zxrX2N-yHEldXPnTW4d_hDB8GDvolw' alt='Israeli army vehicles and bulldozers operate in southern Lebanon, as seen from northern Israel, Wednesday, April 15, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Washington pressure: The Globe and Mail reported that Netanyahu’s government was under heavy pressure from Washington to reach a ceasefire in Lebanon.</li><li>US mediation: The Guardian reported that Washington mediated the rare direct contacts between Israeli and Lebanese representatives.</li><li>Hormuz stakes: The Guardian said the Trump administration was also raising hopes for a broader deal that would open the Strait of Hormuz, underscoring the wider regional implications of the Lebanon diplomacy.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Israeli government: Israeli officials showed openness to a Lebanon 